    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p17-q27-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Find $\\cos\\angle ACB$ in terms of $d$, $\\alpha$, $\\beta$ and $\\theta$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\displaystyle\\cos\\angle ACB=\\frac{\\cos^2\\beta\\cos^2\\theta+\\cos^2\\alpha\\sin^2\\theta-\\cos^2\\alpha\\cos^2\\beta}{2\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta\\cos\\alpha\\cos\\beta}$.\n\nExpress $AC$ and $BC$ using the right-angled triangles, then apply the cosine rule in $\\triangle ACB$; $d$ cancels.",
          "worked": "From the right-angled triangles $OAB$, $OAC$ and $OBC$:\n$$\\begin{align*}OA&=d\\cos\\theta\\\\OB&=d\\sin\\theta\\\\AC&=\\frac{OA}{\\cos\\alpha}\\\\AC&=\\frac{d\\cos\\theta}{\\cos\\alpha}\\\\BC&=\\frac{OB}{\\cos\\beta}\\\\BC&=\\frac{d\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\beta}.\\end{align*}$$\nBy the cosine rule in $\\triangle ACB$:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\cos\\angle ACB&=\\frac{AC^2+BC^2-AB^2}{2(AC)(BC)}\\\\\\cos\\angle ACB&=\\frac{\\dfrac{d^2\\cos^2\\theta}{\\cos^2\\alpha}+\\dfrac{d^2\\sin^2\\theta}{\\cos^2\\beta}-d^2}{\\dfrac{2d^2\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\alpha\\cos\\beta}}\\\\\\cos\\angle ACB&=\\frac{\\cos^2\\beta\\cos^2\\theta+\\cos^2\\alpha\\sin^2\\theta-\\cos^2\\alpha\\cos^2\\beta}{2\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta\\cos\\alpha\\cos\\beta}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 100
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q27-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Show that $$\\cos\\angle ACB=\\frac{\\tan\\theta}{2},$$ given the condition that $\\alpha=\\beta=\\theta$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\displaystyle\\cos\\angle ACB=\\frac{\\tan\\theta}{2}$.\n\nSubstitute $\\alpha=\\beta=\\theta$ into part (a) and use $1-\\cos^2\\theta=\\sin^2\\theta$.",
          "worked": "Substitute $\\alpha=\\beta=\\theta$ into part (a):\n$$\\begin{align*}\\cos\\angle ACB&=\\frac{\\cos^4\\theta+\\cos^2\\theta\\sin^2\\theta-\\cos^4\\theta}{2\\cos^3\\theta\\sin\\theta}\\\\\\cos\\angle ACB&=\\frac{\\cos^2\\theta\\sin^2\\theta}{2\\cos^3\\theta\\sin\\theta}\\\\\\cos\\angle ACB&=\\frac{\\sin\\theta}{2\\cos\\theta}\\\\\\cos\\angle ACB&=\\frac{\\tan\\theta}{2}.\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 48
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q27-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Deduce that if also $\\angle ACB=60^\\circ$, then $\\triangle ACB$ is an equilateral triangle.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\alpha=\\beta=\\theta=45^\\circ$, so $OA=OB=OC$ and $AC=BC=AB=d$. Hence $\\triangle ACB$ is equilateral.\n\nUse part (b), then the three isosceles right-angled component triangles.",
          "worked": "Using part (b):\n$$\\begin{align*}\\tan\\theta&=2\\cos60^\\circ\\\\\\tan\\theta&=1\\\\\\theta&=45^\\circ.\\end{align*}$$\nThus $\\alpha=\\beta=\\theta=45^\\circ$. The right-angled triangle $OAB$ gives $OA=OB$, triangle $OAC$ gives $OA=OC$, and triangle $OBC$ gives $OB=OC$. Therefore $OA=OB=OC$, and\n$$\\begin{align*}AB^2&=OA^2+OB^2=2OA^2\\\\AC^2&=OA^2+OC^2=2OA^2\\\\BC^2&=OB^2+OC^2=2OA^2.\\end{align*}$$\nHence $AC=BC=AB=d$, so $\\triangle ACB$ is equilateral.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 84
      }
    ]
  },
